   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the military law, in relation to in-state tuition continuity\nfor spouses and children of active duty service members",
    "basePrintNo": "S5245",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5245-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1 & 253, Mil L",
    "lawSection": "Military Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5245\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the military law, in relation to in-state tuition conti-\nnuity for spouses and children of active duty service members\n\n \nPURPOSE::\n\nAllows the spouse or child of an active duty service member who is\nassigned to duty elsewhere following assignment in New York to be deemed\nan in-state resident for the purposes of determining tuition and fees at\nall state institutions of higher learning.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends § I of the Military law by adding the\nUnited States Space Force to the list of the military services, a tech-\nnical correction which will guarantee the benefits and protections of\nthe military law are extended to those men and women serving in that\nservice branch.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends § 253 of the Military law to allow the\nspouse or child of an active-duty service member who has been assigned\nto duty elsewhere to be deemed to be an in-state resident for the\npurposes of determining tuition and fees at all state institutions of\nhigher learning, as long as the spouse or child resides continuously in\nNew York and remains enrolled at the institution.\n\nSection 3 is the enacting clause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nEach year, the United States Department of Defense identifies ten key\nissues for states to support the quality of life for service members and\nmilitary families. This legislation enacts the DoD best practices for\nin-state tuition continuity, to allow a spouse or child of an active-\nduty service member, if they have been accepted at an institution of\nhigher learning but their sponsor is transferred to another state, to be\ndeemed an in-state resident as long as they remain continuously enrolled\nat the school.\n\nOut-of-state or nonresident tuition is often two to three times that of\nin-state tuition, and a change in status before enrollment puts the\nstudent in a situation where they must find another college at the last\nminute or pay higher tuition. New York can best support its 11,000\nactive-duty spouses and 17,000 active-duty children by joining the 25\nother states which have enacted this policy since it was identified by\nDoD in 2019.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nMaintaining existing tuition rates creates no additional costs to be\nincurred by the state.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
