    "lawCode": "Amd §§355, 6206 & 6305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5245A\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to in-state tuition\ncontinuity for certain spouses and dependents of active duty service\nmembers\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow the spouse or child of an active\nduty service member who is assigned to duty elsewhere following assign-\nment in New York to be deemed an in-state resident for the purposes of\ndetermining tuition and fees at all state institutions of higher learn-\ning.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the section 355 of the education law to provide\nstudents in-state tuition and fees at SUNY schools who are the spouse or\ndependent of full-time active duty members of the armed forces if they\nhave recently moved out of state as the result of redeployment.\n\nSection 2 amends section 6206 of the education law to provide students\nin-state tuition and fees at CUNY schools who are the spouse or depend-\nent of full-time active duty members of the armed forces if they have\nrecently moved out of state as the result of redeployment.\n\nSection 3 amends section 6305 of the education law to provide students\nin-state tuition and fees at Community Colleges schools who are the\nspouse or dependent of full-time active duty members of the armed forces\nif they have recently moved out of state as the result of redeployment.\n\nSection 4 is the enacting clause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEach year, the United States Department of Defense identifies ten key\nissues for states to support the quality of life for service members and\nmilitary families. This legislation enacts the DoD best practices for\nin-state tuition continuity, to allow a spouse or child of an active-\nduty service member, if they have been accepted at an institution of\nhigher learning but their sponsor is transferred to another state, to be\ndeemed an in-state resident as long as they remain continuously enrolled\nat the school.\n\nOut-of-state or nonresident tuition is often two to three times that of\nin-state tuition, and a change in status before enrollment puts the\nstudent in a situation where they must find another college at the last\nminute or pay higher tuition. New York can best support its 11,000\nactive-duty spouses and 17,000 active-duty children by joining the 25\nother states which have enacted this policy since it was identified by\nDoD in 2019.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on July 1, 2022\n",
